## Proof

Let $g(z) = f(z) - z^2$. Since $f$ is analytic on and inside $C$ and $z^2$ is entire, $g$ is analytic on and inside $C$. On $C$ we have $|z|=1$, so $|z^2|=1$, and the hypothesis gives

$$|g(z)| = |f(z)-z^2| \le |z^2| = 1, \qquad |z|=1.$$

By the maximum modulus principle, $|g(z)| \le 1$ for all $|z|\le 1$.

Since $f'(z) = 2z + g'(z)$, we have

$$f'\!\left(\tfrac14\right) = \tfrac12 + g'\!\left(\tfrac14\right).$$

Thus we must maximize $\left|\tfrac12 + g'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)\right|$ over all analytic $g$ on the unit disk with $|g|\le 1$.

**Upper bound.** By the Schwarz–Pick lemma applied at $a=\tfrac14$,

$$|g'(a)| \le \frac{1-|g(a)|^2}{1-|a|^2} \le \frac{1}{1-|a|^2} = \frac{1}{1-1/16} = \frac{16}{15}.$$

By the triangle inequality,

$$\left|\tfrac12 + g'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)\right| \le \tfrac12 + \left|g'\!\left(\tfrac14\right)\right| \le \tfrac12 + \tfrac{16}{15} = \frac{15+32}{30} = \frac{47}{30}.$$

**Attainment.** Let $g(z) = \dfrac{z-\tfrac14}{1-\tfrac14 z}$, a disk automorphism. Then $|g(z)|=1$ on $|z|=1$ and $|g(z)|\le 1$ inside, so the constraint $|f(z)-z^2|\le 1$ on $C$ is satisfied with equality. Moreover $g\!\left(\tfrac14\right)=0$ and

$$g'(z) = \frac{1-\tfrac14 z + \tfrac14(z-\tfrac14)}{(1-\tfrac14 z)^2}, \qquad g'\!\left(\tfrac14\right) = \frac{1-1/16}{(1-1/16)^2} = \frac{16}{15} > 0.$$

Hence $f'\!\left(\tfrac14\right) = \tfrac12 + \tfrac{16}{15} = \tfrac{47}{30}$, achieving the upper bound.

Therefore the maximum possible value is

$$\boxed{\dfrac{47}{30}}.$$

